zPotential # Closed non-displaced nasal bone fracture from impact to face
Minor Trauma and Personal Injury
Nasal bridge focal tenderness, Transient minor epistaxis, Direct frontal blunt force impact without clinical deformity

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MTI-046
Reason for Visit: Acute facial trauma following a strike, requiring urgent evaluation for septal hematoma, midface fracture, and airway compromise.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a direct blunt force impact to the nose causing epistaxis and localized pain. Urgent bedside assessment was mandated to explicitly rule out a septal hematoma (which causes irreversible cartilage necrosis if missed) and to clear major midface fractures prior to daytime clinical availability.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: A ball hit me directly in the nose; it bled briefly and now the bridge is extremely tender and swelling.
History of Present Illness: Patient provides a clear history of a direct frontal blunt force impact (during community sports) occurring 2 hours ago. This resulted in an acute onset of localized facial pain and swelling strictly confined to the nasal bridge. The patient experienced immediate bilateral minor epistaxis that resolved spontaneously with short-term direct pressure. There is exquisite bony point tenderness upon light palpation of the nasal dorsum. The patient has patent bilateral nasal airways upon forced inspiration, though slightly restricted by mucosal edema.
Functional Status: Fully independent.
Social/Safety Concerns: Minor hindrance to returning to contact sports until medically cleared.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 128/80 | HR: 82 | Temp: 36.8 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 82 Kg
General: Alert, well-appearing, with mild localized swelling to the central face.
Physical Exam: Maxillofacial examination reveals a symmetrical external appearance of the nose without gross lateral deviation, depression, or step-off deformity. There is nasal bridge focal tenderness. Internal speculum examination confirms a complete absence of a boggy, fluctuant septal hematoma; the nasal septum is midline and mucosal lining is intact. Visual acuity is normal, extraocular movements are intact, and there is a total lack of midface (maxillary) instability on clinical testing. Mild bilateral periorbital ecchymosis is beginning to develop.
Environmental Observations: Injury occurred off-site; no immediate indoor hazards.
Medication Reconciliation: No routine anticoagulants.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Potential closed non-displaced nasal bone fracture - Status: Stable
Differentials:

Septal hematoma - Ruled out due to: Direct internal visualization confirming a flat, non-fluctuant, and intact mucosal septum bilaterally.

Orbital blowout fracture - Ruled out due to: Normal extraocular muscle movements, absence of diplopia, and intact sensation over the infraorbital nerve distribution.

Le Fort midface fracture - Ruled out due to: Completely stable hard palate upon manipulation and intact dental occlusion.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

Blunt trauma to the midface carries a high-acuity risk for a septal hematoma and complex orbital fractures. Bedside clinical assessment confidently excluded a septal hematoma via speculum examination, and cleared the orbit and maxilla through functional testing. Urgent after-hours assessment was clinically required to rule out these surgical and functional emergencies, providing safe clearance for conservative management. As the nose is cosmetically straight, standard facial radiographs are clinically unnecessary as they would not alter the acute management plan.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Instructed on strict avoidance of nose blowing to prevent subcutaneous emphysema. Prescribed short-term oral analgesia and applied an ice pack. Advised that routine x-rays are not required for a clinically straight, non-displaced nasal injury.
Positive Behavioral Change: Apply an ice pack wrapped in a cloth to the nasal bridge for 15 minutes every 2 hours. This directly causes local vasoconstriction, which stops the minor mucosal bleeding and actively suppresses the severe periorbital swelling that occurs over the first 24 hours.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): Reusable cold pack.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 5-7 days after swelling subsides to perform a final cosmetic and airway assessment.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present to the Emergency Department immediately if you develop double vision, a sudden severe headache, a clear watery discharge constantly leaking from the nose, or if a soft, painful cherry-red bulge appears inside the nostril.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Nasal swelling masks minor deformities; the final cosmetic appearance can only be evaluated once the swelling completely resolves in 7 days.
Clinical Course: The bruising around the eyes (raccoon eyes) will significantly worsen tomorrow and change color over 2 weeks. The nose will remain tender for up to 4 weeks.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek emergency medical attention if you suddenly cannot breathe through either nostril, or if your vision becomes blurred or doubled.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: A direct hit causes the delicate vessels inside the nose to bleed. The ice physically shrinks these vessels. We do not x-ray straight noses because the treatment (doing nothing but resting) is exactly the same whether it is cracked or not.
Actionable Steps: Do not blow your nose for 48 hours; dab it gently. Sleep with your head propped up on two pillows to reduce swelling. Use the ice packs as directed.

